Name the object file each compile rule is meant to produce.

Most of the distribution's compile rules pass no -o and rely on the
compiler's default output name:

    sunvox_engine.o: $(SUNVOX)sunvox_engine.cpp $(SUNVOX_DEPS) $(SD_DEPS)
    	$(CXX) $(FINAL_CFLAGS) -c $(SUNVOX)sunvox_engine.cpp

A GNU-style driver writes sunvox_engine.o there and the rule is satisfied
by accident. clang-cl follows MSVC and writes sunvox_engine.obj, so the
target is never created. Make does not notice -- the recipe succeeded --
and the build fails much later at the archive step, with nothing in the
log to say which compile was responsible:

    llvm-ar.exe: error: sunvox_engine.o: no such file or directory
    make: *** [../../lib_sundog/sundog_makefile.inc:737: all] Error 1

It failed for some objects and not others because the rules are
inconsistent: everything under lib_vorbis and lib_flac already passes an
explicit -o, which is why the tremor and FLAC objects came out fine while
lib_sundog, lib_sunvox and lib_dsp produced nothing usable.

This adds "-o $@" to the 122 rules that lacked it, so every rule names
the file it is declared to build. It is the same name the GNU drivers
were already choosing, so nothing changes for the Linux, macOS or MinGW
builds; it only stops the output name from being the compiler's guess.

